dist/
coverage.lcov

# Playwright E2E artefacts
test-results/
playwright-report/

# Marketing screenshot pipeline output (binaries, regenerated each run)
screenshots-out/
# Local, private banned-token overrides for the sanitization gate (never committed)
scripts/screenshots/.banned-tokens.json

# Release gate record (per-machine, references a local SHA)
.release-gate.json

# Review records are NOT tracked. They were, and the reason they stopped is
# worth keeping: each row cites the hash of the criteria it was judged against,
# and those criteria are not in this repository, so no reader with only a clone
# can resolve the hash to anything. A record nobody outside can verify is not a
# record, it is an artefact of somebody else's process.
#
# They live with the criteria they cite, and the fact of review stays here in
# the form a reader can actually use: the pull request states the verdict, the
# models and what was judged, in prose.
.independent-review/

# Local pre-commit gate record: names the tree the checks passed on.
.precommit-gate.json

# Written while a mutation harness holds a source file broken, and removed when
# it puts the file back. It survives only a run that was killed outright, which
# is exactly when it is read: the next run stops and names the files that may
# still hold a mutation. Ignored because a record of a run that went wrong must
# never itself be the thing `git add -A` sweeps into a commit.
.mutation-run.json

# Per-workspace scratch and local state Rundock writes into a workspace it has
# opened. A checkout is itself a plausible workspace, so this directory appears
# here during ordinary use and during development, and nothing in it belongs to
# the project. Added after finding that contributor instructions said to put
# scratch files here while `git add -A` would have committed them: the guidance
# and the mechanism disagreed, and only the habit of deleting kept the two from
# meeting.
.rundock/
